	title = {Evaluation of Welding Residual Stress in a TC4 Titanium Alloy Square Tube Using Ultrasonic Non-Destructive Testing},
	year = {2025},
	journal = {Proceedings of 2025 6th International Conference on Mechanical Automation and Intelligent Manufacturing, MAIM 2025},
	pages = {1 - 6},
	doi = {10.1109/MAIM68393.2025.11470074},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105037575905?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Residual stresses induced during welding can significantly affect the structural integrity and performance of titanium alloy components, particularly in aerospace and highprecision applications. This study investigates the evolution of residual stress in a TC4 titanium alloy square tube using ultrasonic non-destructive testing (NDT) based on the acoustoelastic effect. Residual stress measurements were conducted both parallel and perpendicular to the weld seam across three fabrication stages: post-welding, post-heat treatment, and post-fixture removal. The results show that fixture removal was more effective than heat treatment in redistributing residual stresses, with localized stress reductions exceeding 40 MPa. While heat treatment reduced stress concentration, its effects were more limited in specific regions, particularly on the right side of the weld. Statistical analysis through box plots revealed directional and positional dependencies in stress distribution, highlighting the sensitivity and reliability of multi-stage ultrasonic evaluation. These findings provide practical insights for optimizing welding parameters, thermal cycles, and fixture removal strategies, with potential applications in titanium alloy fabrication. Future work will integrate real-time monitoring and destructive validation to further enhance the robustness and accuracy of ultrasonic residual stress assessment.  © 2025 IEEE.},
