	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105045600216?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Requirements-based testing often starts from requirements written in natural language, but test design usually needs an explicit model of the decision logic so that test conditions can be checked and discussed. Cause-effect graphs (CEGs) provide such a model by linking input conditions and outcomes, and they can be used both to derive test cases and to support requirements review. In practice, however, constructing a CEG is time-consuming and requires expertise, and existing automation techniques tend to assume templates, rules, or semi-structured inputs, which limits their use with free-form requirements text. This paper presents CEGen, an LLM-assisted method for translating natural-language requirements into CEGs. CEGen does not ask an LLM to output graphs directly. Instead, the LLM extracts causes and effects and populates truth tables and constraints from requirement statements. CEGen then constructs CEGs deterministically from these intermediate representations. To facilitate inspection, CEGen generates multiple equivalent CEGs from alternative simplified logical forms, which can change the visual structure even when the logic is identical. We evaluated CEGen on ten problems from a tester training book, running the method five times per problem. CEGen produced fully correct CEGs (correct causes, effects/logic, and constraints) in 52% of the runs. Developers reported that inspecting a generated CEG can be faster than drawing one from scratch, and that the preferred representation varies across individuals, with the hybrid form often selected when available. We also discuss failure modes, limitations of the evaluation, and the scope of applicability.  © 2026 IEEE.},
