	pages = {},
	doi = {10.1109/GSEACT68539.2026.11620126},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105047866933?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {IoT devices have been quickly expanding these days, bringing about serious security concerns with ever-changing network intrusions. Static detection strategies consider individual network flows rather than how attack behaviors change over time. In this paper, we design a temporal intrusion detection system that captures representations of consecutive few IoT network flows rather than classifying individual network flows separately. We construct a model with convolutional neural networks (CNNs) to extract spatial features, bidirectional long short-term memory networks (BiLSTM) to capture sequential dependencies between features, and an attention layer that learns to focus on critical time segments. Traffic generated from IoT is classified into four classes: normal, DDoS-ACK fragmentation attack, dictionary brute force attack, and browser hijacking attack. Our experiments on the CICIoT2023 dataset report an accuracy of about 98.24% and high precision and recall for all four classes. We show that modeling short-term temporal dependencies leads to better detection performance on contemporary IoT networks. © 2026 IEEE.},
