	abstract = {Large Language Models (LLMs) have recently been proposed as a means to automate software test case generation from natural language requirements. Beyond single-shot prompting, recent work advocates agentic LLM architectures, in which multiple coordinated roles (e.g., planner, generator, critic, refiner) iteratively produce and improve test artifacts. However, the empirical benefits of such agentic approaches relative to their computational cost remain underexplored. In this paper, we empirically evaluate three LLM-based pipelines for requirements-based test case generation: (1) a single-shot baseline, (2) a multi-step decomposition pipeline, and (3) a fully agentic pipeline with iterative critique and refinement. Using a real-world Bluetooth Video Distribution Profile (VDP) specification and its official conformance test suite, we compare generated tests against reference tests using deterministic quality, coverage, and similarity metrics, while also measuring inference cost in terms of latency, token usage, and number of model calls. Our results show that multi-step decomposition improves similarity to reference tests by up to 24% over single-shot prompting, while agentic refinement provides only marginal gains at a substantially higher cost–incurring up to 30× more tokens and 24× higher latency. These findings suggest that, for structured and well-specified requirements, lightweight planning is sufficient, and more complex agentic architectures may not be cost-effective. © The Author(s), under exclusive license to Springer Nature Switzerland AG 2027.},
