	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105044273747?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Traditional smart home systems typically employ fixed control logic or rely on users' active commands to adjust environmental parameters, making it difficult to adapt to the dynamic changes in users' daily behavior patterns and their implicit needs. This paper proposes an adaptive control framework for indoor spaces that is driven by user behavior and integrates multimodal perception with deep reinforcement learning techniques. The framework collects human activity, posture information, and local microenvironment data via sensors in real-world scenarios. It utilizes a hybrid multimodal attention network to extract spatiotemporal behavioral features, which are then fed into a deep reinforcement learning agent to coordinate the adjustment of lighting, shading, and indoor temperature. The reward function balances the dual objectives of user comfort and system energy consumption. Experimental results from a realworld smart home test bed demonstrate that the proposed framework achieves a user satisfaction rate of 93.2% and reduces overall energy consumption by 26.8% compared to traditional methods. This research provides a new technical pathway for natural, seamless interaction between humans, machines, and the environment, and holds significant application potential in scenarios such as senior housing, smart homes, and smart offices.  © 2026 IEEE.},
