	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105042568880?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {This paper proposes a behavior-driven methodology for the systematic optimization and tuning of training steps in machine learning models. Starting from conventional static hyperparameter approaches, we propose a framework that decomposes the training process into more controllable stages, including initialization, learning rate scheduling, and adaptive adjustments guided by behavioral indicators derived from training dynamics, such as gradient variance and loss evolution. Our approach frames training as a closed-loop control system using multiple behavioral signals to steer hyperparameter adaptation during training. The tuning is cast as a multi-objective optimization process balancing accuracy with stability and generalization also computational efficiency. The framework monitor in real time behavior of the training and adapt dynamically parameters and improves convergence stability, also reduces performance variance and enhances generalization as soon as it detected inefficiencies. The results of the experiments show performance improvements across conventional convolutional neurol networks or more complex models such as MobileNet, ResNet and RetinaNet. The proposed model-agnostic framework provides a practical and lightweight contribution to the optimization of deep learning training pipelines. © 2026 IEEE.},
