	abstract = {Pedestrian trajectory prediction is crucial for intelligent transportation and autonomous driving, as its accuracy significantly impacts system safety and efficiency. Existing methods often rely on static thresholds for constructing social interaction graphs, limiting their ability to adapt to dynamic social attributes in complex scenes, thus reducing prediction accuracy. This paper proposes the Abrupt Behavior Driven Interaction Attention Model (AB-DIAM), a novel framework that effectively addresses social interaction modeling challenges by utilizing the proposed modules: Dynamic Social Interaction Range Adjustment (DSIRA), Dynamic Behavior Embedded Social Interaction (DBESI), and Abrupt Behavior Interaction Attention (ABIA). Specifically, DSIRA employs an adaptive dynamic threshold graph construction algorithm driven by changes in pedestrian speed and direction, which enhances scene adaptability. DBESI embeds behavioral features into node representations to build a hypergraph, enabling robust social interaction modeling. ABIA dynamically adjusts attention weight to capture short-term abrupt behaviors, which optimizes feature extraction and improves accuracy in trajectory prediction. Experimental results on public datasets show that the proposed AB-DIAM outperforms state-of-the-art baselines in Average Displacement Error (ADE) and Final Displacement Error (FDE). © 2026 Elsevier B.V.},
