	abstract = {The paper describes requirement based system testing methodology for the application of safety-critical systems. It mainly describes applicable Code and Standard Framework, establishment of testbed, test methodology, test case design, test procedures and paradigm of sample execution using testbed. Largely, there are two regulatory frameworks USNRC based on the IEEE standard and IAEA-based IEC standard. In the process of developing requirement based system testing, we met the USNRC based Code and Standard criteria. The criteria of safety-critical software qualification are based on the NUREG-0800/BTP-14(Revision 5 - March 2007) where the most recent edition is used for each design output and testing. In general, software lifecycle consists of requirement phase, design phase, implementation phase, testing phase, integration phase(software-software, software-hardware), installation phase, operation and maintenance phase. Fundamental parts to testing phase are component test, software integration test, software-hardware integration test, and system test. Among these tests, especially, we focused on the requirement based system test. Testing software or hardware requires completely integrated system and its purpose is to evaluate the compliance of system with the specified requirements. The system test operates functionality, performance and interface because system testing falls within the scope of black box testing which should not require knowledge of the inner design of the code or logic. When numerous test approach are present, 100% coverage all the tests manually remains as a challenge. The purpose of developing requirement based system test using testbed is to save time and efforts from functionality and performance tests. We developed the testbed like I/O stimulator for burning test based on operational scenario. Hardware, software, and man-machine-interface (MMI) are fundamental parts to the testbed. Signals sources from the testbed contain analog input, analog output, digital input and digital output, Programmable Power Supply, RS232C Serial interface and dual port Ethernet interface. Using Lab VIEW programming instead of test scripts, we established the testbed MMI control station that can generate signal sources such as tri-angle wave, sign wave, and step-wise wave. The testbed can control not only the real-time condition parameters, but also weighted signal test. In particular, weighted signal test is very important for instrumentation and control devices in nuclear facilities. The testbed can provide a solution for the time discrepancy between input signal source and result value during real-time synchronization process. Before testing start, test procedures were setup in advance, we performed the test scenario-based automatic functional testing for safety-grade digital device as a thirty-party verifier. It was successfully on prototype requirement based testbed setup. While automation cannot reproduce everything that a software engineer can do, it can be extremely useful for the system test. However, it does require a well-developed test suite of testing scripts in order to be useful. In this study, we developed the multipurpose and cost-efficient testbed using Lab VIEW program instead of testing script.},
